  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 353 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nPeriodic \nprecipitation chances continue to be the main forecast challenge in \nthe long term. Upper level low pressure will track from the Pacific \nNW into the northern Rockies Sunday into Sunday night. In advance of \nthis feature, a lead shortwave will approach the central Rockies \nSunday afternoon. As this feature interacts with a narrow band of \nfavorable theta-e and moisture along the front ranges of \nColorado and Wyoming, thunderstorms are expected to develop in \nthese areas late Sunday afternoon into the early evening hours. \nSouthwesterly winds will carry this activity east into western \nNebraska Sunday evening. A low level jet, oriented from Kansas \nnorth into the Nebraska Sandhills, will help to sustain \nconvection into the overnight. ATTM based on the latest \ndeterministic GFS and NAM12 solns, along with the NBM ensemble \nforecast, areas across the Sandhills into north central Nebraska\nstand to see the best chances for precipitation Sunday night \ninto Monday morning. In the wake of precipitation Monday, upper \nlevel low pressure will track across southern Saskatchewan into \nsouthern Manitoba. Deepening surface low pressure in association\nwith the upper level low, will force a cold front into the \nwestern Dakotas Monday. This feature will approach northwestern \nNebraska Monday afternoon, pushing through the remainder of the \nforecast area by Monday evening. In advance of this feature, \nvery warm air ahead of the front will lead to highs in the 80s \nwith near 90 in the southwest. In a setup similar to the last 12\nhours, westerly winds in advance of the frontal boundary, will \nlimit low level moisture Monday, focusing the threat for \nprecipitation INVOF the front Monday evening. That being said, \nthe best chances for precipitation Monday night into Tuesday \nwill be along the cold front. This favors the southern half of \nthe forecast area. As for severe chances, low level moisture \nremains meager Monday-even in the southeastern forecast area. \nLimited CAPE is noted Monday afternoon/evening in the southeast.\nGiven the meager amount of CAPE, believe the severe threat will\nbe limited at best. Overnight Monday night, the front will be \nforced south into Kansas where it will stall for the next 24 to \n36 hours. This will limit precipitation chances Tuesday into \nTuesday night across the area. By Wednesday, a deepening trough \nof low pressure aloft over the intermountain west, will begin to\ndraw the front north bringing better precipitation chances to \nthe southern half of the forecast area Wednesday into Wednesday \nnight. These chances will continue Thursday into Friday as the \nupper level trough across the intermountain west, slowly \ntransitions east northeast into the northern Rockies.",
